<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/expert/genomic-medicine">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Genomic medicine uses <strong>a patient's DNA as clinical data</strong> — for diagnosis,
    drug selection, risk prediction and family planning. The Human Genome Project's promise is
    arriving unevenly: spectacular in cancer pharmacogenomics and rare diseases, still
    maturing for common complex diseases — with interpretation and ethics as the bottlenecks,
    not the sequencing.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four clinical uses and their open problems" widget="equationStep" config={genomeCfg} />

  <h2>Where genomics already changed outcomes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rare disease diagnosis:</strong> patients with mysterious conditions endured "diagnostic odysseys" of 5–10 years; exome sequencing ends many of them in weeks — naming the disease, sometimes enabling targeted treatment.</li>
    <li><strong>Cancer immunotherapy selection:</strong> tumour mutational burden and mismatch-repair status predict checkpoint-inhibitor response — genomically selected patients get dramatically better outcomes.</li>
    <li><strong>Prenatal and newborn screening:</strong> non-invasive prenatal testing (sequencing foetal DNA from maternal blood, 2011→) replaced risky amniocentesis for many; expanded newborn sequencing raises the storage/consent questions early in life.</li>
    <li><strong>Population-scale biobanks:</strong> UK Biobank (500,000 volunteers), All of Us (1 million) — linking genomes to health records at scale, finding associations medicine never knew to look for.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Human Genome Project (Nature 2001); UK Biobank publications; NCGEN guidelines; Casgevy approval (2023) for CRISPR therapy.</p>
</TopicPage>
